From 92e3135eddb4ea723dcf64f45e82175040545054 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:12:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(sessions): single-flight + data-stamp cache for GET
 /api/sessions [daveey]

hermes-hub: Desktop reconnect bursts of ~15 identical /api/sessions calls each
ran the recent-order list SQL (~0.1-0.3 s on a 7 GB state.db) plus the activity
annotations in its own threadpool slot; the burst queued behind itself (2-7 s
per call, ~3,000 slow calls in 30 h, 131 requests parked in run_in_threadpool).

Identical calls (same params + state.db path) now share one computation while
one is in flight, and a finished result is reused for 2 s. The cache key's data
stamp is SQLite's WAL-index header (iChange/mxFrame/salts, both copies must
agree) plus stat of the db and WAL, so any committed write to state.db misses at
once; the TTL only bounds staleness of annotations read from other stores. No
WAL/shm means no caching or coalescing (old behaviour). Each caller gets its own
copy of the payload rows.

Measured on the hub's live 7.2 GB state.db, 15 concurrent identical calls:
uncached p95 2.14 s (15 SQL runs) -> 0.15 s (1 SQL run).
